nvpmodel: tegra210: cap the memory clock through bwmgr

nvpmodel caps the memory clock for each power mode through
/sys/kernel/nvpmodel_emc_cap. R32's driver registers a bandwidth manager
client on every chip; R35's does so only on Tegra194 and otherwise asks the
interconnect framework for a path Tegra210 does not provide. Its init
failed, the sysfs file never appeared, and the nvpmodel service failed. Its
error path also called icc_put() on an error pointer, which is the call
trace logged in every 5.10 boot until this patch.

Tegra210 now takes the Tegra194 path; the bandwidth manager works on
Tegra210 from 0010.

Found: trial 17. Tested on the Nano: in trial 27 the driver initialized
with no call trace; with the corrected nvpmodel config line (see
README.md) the 5W and MAXN modes set the same limits as under 4.9 in
trial 29.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
---
diff --git a/nvidia/drivers/nvpmodel/nvpmodel_emc_cap.c b/nvidia/drivers/nvpmodel/nvpmodel_emc_cap.c
index 2f676c139..bcfef27e3 100644
--- a/nvidia/drivers/nvpmodel/nvpmodel_emc_cap.c
+++ b/nvidia/drivers/nvpmodel/nvpmodel_emc_cap.c
@@ -217,7 +217,12 @@ static int __init nvpmodel_clk_cap_init(void)
 		goto exit;
 	}
 
-	if (tegra_get_chip_id() == TEGRA194) {
+	/*
+	 * Tegra210 has no interconnect provider here: cap the EMC through the
+	 * bandwidth manager, as R32's driver did on every chip.
+	 */
+	if (tegra_get_chip_id() == TEGRA194 ||
+	    tegra_get_chip_id() == TEGRA210) {
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_TEGRA_BWMGR)
 		bwmgr_handle =
 			tegra_bwmgr_register(TEGRA_BWMGR_CLIENT_NVPMODEL);
